ABCL — Probability Collapse

ABCL635’s positive Phase 2 hot-flash results changed the probability distribution around the drug rather than merely improving quarterly revenue. The stock surged sharply after the data, with favorable tolerability and efficacy among the reported results. 

Pattern:

\boxed{
\text{large uncertainty}
\rightarrow
\text{new hard information}
\rightarrow
\text{uncertainty collapses}
\rightarrow
\text{rapid repricing}
}

That’s fundamentally different from an ordinary earnings beat.

Call it Probability Collapse.

Biotech is full of it.

And this pattern should transfer to:

* regulatory decisions;
* court rulings;
* exploration results;
* product approvals;
* election outcomes;
* binary technical milestones.

⸻

MPC — Bottleneck Migration

The interesting discovery wasn’t simply “oil goes up → refiner goes up.”

Refinery capacity itself became constrained. Disruptions reduced refined-product supply, causing diesel/fuel prices and refining margins to rise faster than crude itself. 

So:

\text{shock}
\rightarrow
\text{scarcity}
\rightarrow
\text{bottleneck moves}
\rightarrow
\text{profit pool moves}

That’s an exceptionally transferable operator.

Don’t merely ask:

What’s scarce?

Ask:

Where did the bottleneck migrate?

